description:
The app has NO React Error Boundary. If any component throws during rendering (corrupted localStorage state, null reference in node data, unexpected edge data shape), the entire app crashes to a white screen with no recovery path.

For a live demo, this is a critical risk: one unexpected error in a single node's rendering would kill the entire presentation.

Fix: Add a React Error Boundary component that wraps the App. On error, show a friendly fallback UI with:
1. A clear message ('Something went wrong')
2. A 'Reload' button that refreshes the page
3. Optionally a 'Clear data and reload' button that clears localStorage (in case corrupted data caused the crash)

Minimal implementation:
```tsx
// src/components/ui/error-boundary.tsx
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  state = { hasError: false, error: null };
  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) { return { hasError: true, error }; }
  componentDidCatch(error, info) { console.error('App crash:', error, info); }
  render() { return this.state.hasError ? <FallbackUI /> : this.props.children; }
}
```

Wrap in main.tsx: `<ErrorBoundary><BrowserRouter><App />...</BrowserRouter></ErrorBoundary>`

This is a 15-minute task that provides critical demo safety.
